# A run started from the dashboard («شغّل»): enqueue the sheet rows, then work the queue (main.py --worker
# --trigger=dashboard), as the app user. Started by laqta-run.path, never by hand and never by a timer.
#
# Why this exists: php-fpm used to start the worker itself (nohup sh -c ... &) inside php-fpm's own cgroup, so an
# unattended php-fpm restart or package upgrade killed a run in progress. Here the run lives in this unit's cgroup:
# `systemctl restart php8.3-fpm` does not touch it. The stop button keeps working as before (the worker's pid and
# heartbeat are in temp/pipeline.lock, php-fpm runs as the same user and may stop it); `systemctl stop
# laqta-run` stops it from a shell. Output goes to temp/pipeline.log, which the dashboard rotates at each start.
# The nightly run (laqta-nightly.service) is not affected: both use the same temp/pipeline.lock, only one can work.
# MemoryMax is filled in by install.sh like in laqta-nightly.service; a run killed for memory is the only failure and
# the dashboard shows it in the run state. Exit codes: killed by the stop button (KILL) or stopped on purpose (TERM)
# are normal. No OnFailure alert here: a stop would raise a false alarm, and the run report goes out by itself.
# The @NAME@ words are filled in by install.sh.
[Unit]
Description=Laqta run started from the dashboard (main.py --enqueue, then --worker)
After=network-online.target mariadb.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=@APP_USER@
Group=@APP_USER@
WorkingDirectory=@APP_DIR@
Environment=PYTHONUTF8=1
Environment=PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8
Environment=U2NET_HOME=@MODELS_DIR@
ExecStart=/bin/bash @APP_DIR@/deploy/ubuntu/run-launcher.sh @APP_DIR@
# 3 = stopped on purpose (graceful stop); KILL = the dashboard's stop button after its own wait; TERM = a plain stop.
SuccessExitStatus=3 KILL TERM
# Hard backstop (a run is hours at most): a hung run must not block the launcher for ever.
TimeoutStartSec=12h
# A stop (systemctl stop, a reboot, the dashboard) sends SIGTERM and the process shuts down gracefully: it finishes the
# product in hand (up to its 45 s grace, SHUTDOWN_GRACE_S), writes the report and the Telegram message, and exits with
# code 3 ("stopped on purpose"). TimeoutStopSec is the wait before systemd sends SIGKILL: well above the grace.
KillSignal=SIGTERM
TimeoutStopSec=120
Nice=5
MemoryMax=@MEMORY_MAX@
OOMPolicy=kill
OOMScoreAdjust=300
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
ProtectSystem=full
